WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.497
In the last part we saw what

00:00:02.576 --> 00:00:03.988
partition must do.

00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:05.636
Now, how it does it.

00:00:05.636 --> 00:00:08.696
This version, named after Nico Lomuto

00:00:08.696 --> 00:00:11.992
scans the range once, from left to right.

00:00:11.992 --> 00:00:14.660
First it records the pivot value.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.952
Then it parks the pivot at the

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.541
right end of the range, out of the way.

00:00:19.541 --> 00:00:21.871
nextSmallPos starts at left

00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:24.137
it is where the next value smaller

00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:25.690
than the pivot will go.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.690
Index j runs from left to right - 1.

00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:32.383
Whenever arr[j] is smaller

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.295
than the pivot value

00:00:34.295 --> 00:00:37.597
it is swapped to nextSmallPos

00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:41.160
and nextSmallPos moves on by 1.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.549
After the loop, one last swap puts the pivot

00:00:44.618 --> 00:00:46.206
at nextSmallPos

00:00:46.206 --> 00:00:47.795
between the two parts

00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:50.350
and partition returns that position.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.032
During the loop the range is

00:00:53.108 --> 00:00:55.060
divided into four regions.

00:00:55.135 --> 00:00:57.763
From left up to nextSmallPos

00:00:57.838 --> 00:01:00.466
are the values seen so far that are

00:01:00.541 --> 00:01:02.268
smaller than the pivot.

00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:05.347
From nextSmallPos up to j are the

00:01:05.422 --> 00:01:08.050
values seen so far that are larger.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.338
From j to right - 1: not yet examined.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:15.560
And at right sits the parked pivot.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.009
When arr[j] is small

00:01:18.093 --> 00:01:20.193
swapping it with

00:01:20.276 --> 00:01:23.887
arr[nextSmallPos], the first large value,

00:01:23.971 --> 00:01:26.742
and advancing nextSmallPos

00:01:26.826 --> 00:01:29.513
extends the small region by 1

00:01:29.513 --> 00:01:32.956
and keeps the large region in one piece.

00:01:32.956 --> 00:01:35.139
When arr[j] is large

00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:38.750
just moving j on extends the large region.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.030
At the end, nextSmallPos is the

00:01:42.107 --> 00:01:43.726
first large position.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.270
Swapping the pivot there puts it

00:01:46.347 --> 00:01:48.660
right between the two regions.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.477
In Java it is the same, line for line.

00:01:52.477 --> 00:01:56.139
Record the pivot value and park the pivot.

00:01:56.139 --> 00:01:59.120
Start nextSmallPos at left.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.776
The for loop runs j from left

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:04.002
while j is less than right

00:02:04.081 --> 00:02:06.307
and tests arr[j] against

00:02:06.387 --> 00:02:07.659
the pivot value.

00:02:07.738 --> 00:02:09.646
A small value is swapped

00:02:09.726 --> 00:02:13.224
to nextSmallPos, which then moves on.

00:02:13.224 --> 00:02:16.483
Finally the pivot is swapped into place

00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:18.630
and its index is returned.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.244
The slides write this test once

00:02:21.317 --> 00:02:22.851
with the pivot value

00:02:22.924 --> 00:02:24.897
and once as arr[j]

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:26.430
< arr[right].

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.914
They are the same: after parking

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:31.836
the pivot value sits at arr[right]

00:02:31.836 --> 00:02:34.100
and the loop never touches it.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.988
Let us trace the slides' example:

00:02:38.988 --> 00:02:42.024
4, 2, 3, 6, 9, 5, 7

00:02:42.024 --> 00:02:44.374
with pivot index 3.

00:02:44.374 --> 00:02:46.724
The pivot value is 6

00:02:46.724 --> 00:02:50.151
and it is parked at the right end

00:02:50.151 --> 00:02:52.795
7 and 6 swap places.

00:02:52.795 --> 00:02:56.320
nextSmallPos starts at 0.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.098
j is 0: 4 is small.

00:02:59.098 --> 00:03:01.234
It swaps with itself

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:04.827
and nextSmallPos becomes 1.

00:03:04.827 --> 00:03:08.712
j is 1: 2 is small, the same again.

00:03:08.712 --> 00:03:11.237
j is 2: 3 is small

00:03:11.237 --> 00:03:14.150
nextSmallPos is 3.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.117
j is 3: 7 is large.

00:03:17.117 --> 00:03:20.829
Skip; nextSmallPos stays at 3.

00:03:20.829 --> 00:03:23.127
j is 4: 9 is large.

00:03:23.127 --> 00:03:24.188
Skip again.

00:03:24.188 --> 00:03:27.370
The large block is now 7, 9.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.776
j is 5: 5 is small.

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:32.363
It swaps with arr[3]

00:03:32.363 --> 00:03:34.346
the first large value

00:03:34.346 --> 00:03:37.192
5 jumps over the large block

00:03:37.192 --> 00:03:40.468
and nextSmallPos becomes 4.

00:03:40.468 --> 00:03:43.314
While all the values were small

00:03:43.314 --> 00:03:46.246
each swap was a swap with itself.

00:03:46.246 --> 00:03:48.660
This is the first real one.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.781
The loop is over.

00:03:50.781 --> 00:03:55.158
The pivot swaps with arr[4]:

00:03:55.158 --> 00:03:58.909
4, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 9.

00:03:58.909 --> 00:04:01.410
Partition returns 4.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:03.016
Your turn.

00:04:03.016 --> 00:04:06.709
10, 80, 30, 90, 40

00:04:06.811 --> 00:04:09.683
with the pivot 40 already

00:04:09.786 --> 00:04:13.376
at the right end, pivot index 4.

00:04:13.376 --> 00:04:16.966
Write down j, nextSmallPos

00:04:16.966 --> 00:04:20.146
and the array after each step.

00:04:20.146 --> 00:04:23.019
What does partition return?

00:04:23.019 --> 00:04:25.070
Pause and trace it.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.740
nextSmallPos starts at 0.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.918
10 is small: it swaps with itself

00:04:34.918 --> 00:04:37.389
nextSmallPos is 1.

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:38.890
80 is large.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.775
30 is small: it swaps with 80

00:04:42.775 --> 00:04:45.453
giving 10, 30, 80

00:04:45.453 --> 00:04:48.514
and nextSmallPos is 2.

00:04:48.514 --> 00:04:50.140
90 is large.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.157
Finally the pivot swaps with arr[2]

00:04:54.157 --> 00:04:58.192
which is 80:

00:04:58.192 --> 00:04:58.909
10, 30, 40, 90, 80.

00:04:58.909 --> 00:05:02.766
Partition returns 2, the index of 40.

00:05:02.855 --> 00:05:05.366
Here is the program's output

00:05:05.456 --> 00:05:07.070
for both examples.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.308
A quick check for any partition trace:

00:05:10.384 --> 00:05:13.120
the returned index equals the number

00:05:13.196 --> 00:05:16.540
of values smaller than the pivot, plus left.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.668
Two values, 10 and 30,

00:05:18.668 --> 00:05:20.492
are smaller than 40,

00:05:20.492 --> 00:05:23.380
so the answer must be 2, not 3.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.572
Now the whole randomized quicksort in Java.

00:05:27.572 --> 00:05:29.840
If left is at least right,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.200
there are 0 or 1 elements: return.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:37.619
The pivot index is

00:05:37.714 --> 00:05:40.836
left + rng.nextInt(right - left + 1).

00:05:40.931 --> 00:05:43.959
nextInt returns an integer from

00:05:44.054 --> 00:05:46.419
0 to right - left,

00:05:46.514 --> 00:05:49.353
so the sum is a uniform random

00:05:49.447 --> 00:05:51.813
index from left to right,

00:05:51.813 --> 00:05:55.030
exactly RANDOM(left, right).

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:56.763
Partition around it,

00:05:56.832 --> 00:05:59.252
and sort the values below the pivot

00:05:59.321 --> 00:06:00.980
and the values above it.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.232
To watch the recursion,

00:06:03.232 --> 00:06:05.776
the notes print one line per call

00:06:05.776 --> 00:06:08.248
on the 10 numbers from part 1.

00:06:08.248 --> 00:06:09.702
With seed 19,

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.955
the first pivot is 65,

00:06:11.955 --> 00:06:13.918
as in the slides' picture.

00:06:13.918 --> 00:06:16.680
65 is now in its final place.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:21.311
Left part, 0 to 5: pivot 31.

00:06:21.311 --> 00:06:24.369
Then 0 to 2: pivot 0.

00:06:24.369 --> 00:06:27.630
And 1 to 2: pivot 13.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:30.856
4 to 5: pivot 43.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:33.553
On the right, 7 to 9:

00:06:33.553 --> 00:06:35.226
pivot 92.

00:06:35.226 --> 00:06:38.760
And 7 to 8: pivot 81.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:39.523
Sorted.

00:06:39.523 --> 00:06:42.528
26, 57 and 75

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:43.921
were never pivots;

00:06:43.921 --> 00:06:47.146
each ended up alone in a range of size 1,

00:06:47.219 --> 00:06:49.565
and calls on ranges of size 0

00:06:49.638 --> 00:06:52.570
or 1 return at once, printing nothing.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.986
Same input, seed 2026.

00:06:55.986 --> 00:06:59.633
The first pivot is 0, the smallest value.

00:06:59.714 --> 00:07:02.145
The left part is empty and the

00:07:02.226 --> 00:07:05.630
right part has 9 elements: a bad split.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.813
1 to 9: pivot 65.

00:07:08.813 --> 00:07:11.551
1 to 5: pivot 13.

00:07:11.551 --> 00:07:13.911
2 to 5: 31.

00:07:13.911 --> 00:07:16.460
4 to 5: 43.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.256
Then 75 and 81

00:07:19.336 --> 00:07:20.370
on the right.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.280
The same sorted result

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:25.542
but the recursion went one level deeper.

00:07:25.542 --> 00:07:28.804
Same input, same result, different work

00:07:28.804 --> 00:07:31.350
that is a randomized algorithm.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.144
And the question from part one

00:07:34.144 --> 00:07:37.535
how many times can one element be a pivot?

00:07:37.535 --> 00:07:38.639
At most once.

00:07:38.718 --> 00:07:40.689
After partition the pivot

00:07:40.768 --> 00:07:42.503
is in its final place

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:45.420
and both recursive calls exclude it.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.861
Merge sort and quicksort are

00:07:47.928 --> 00:07:49.523
both divide-and-conquer

00:07:49.523 --> 00:07:52.381
but they put the work in different places.

00:07:52.381 --> 00:07:54.375
Merge sort divides trivially

00:07:54.375 --> 00:07:56.103
by cutting in the middle

00:07:56.103 --> 00:07:58.761
quicksort's real work is the partition.

00:07:58.761 --> 00:08:01.220
Merge sort's real work is the merge

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.480
quicksort has nothing to combine.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:07.272
Merging needs a temporary array of size n

00:08:07.272 --> 00:08:10.468
partition swaps inside the same array

00:08:10.468 --> 00:08:11.287
in place.

00:08:11.287 --> 00:08:13.746
Merge sort is O(n log n) always

00:08:13.746 --> 00:08:16.286
quicksort is O(n log n) expected

00:08:16.286 --> 00:08:18.990
and quadratic in the worst case.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:22.548
Yet in practice quicksort is typically

00:08:22.633 --> 00:08:24.828
two to three times faster.

00:08:24.828 --> 00:08:27.276
The constant factors decide

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.906
partition is a simple sequential scan with

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.861
few data moves and no extra array

00:08:33.861 --> 00:08:36.140
which suits the CPU cache.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:37.616
To sum up.

00:08:37.616 --> 00:08:40.483
One: the Lomuto partition parks

00:08:40.573 --> 00:08:43.529
the pivot at right, scans j once

00:08:43.619 --> 00:08:45.859
and swaps small values to

00:08:45.948 --> 00:08:47.740
nextSmallPos.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.682
Two: the pivot ends at nextSmallPos

00:08:51.682 --> 00:08:55.535
which equals the number of smaller values

00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:56.520
plus left.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:59.830
Three: randomized quicksort gives the

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.910
same result on every run

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:03.590
with different work

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.950
and each element is a pivot at most once.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:11.501
Try this: partition

00:09:11.501 --> 00:09:13.697
3, 8, 2, 5, 1, 4, 7, 6

00:09:13.697 --> 00:09:15.892
with pivot index 5.

00:09:15.892 --> 00:09:19.043
Write the array after each step

00:09:19.043 --> 00:09:21.143
and the return value.

00:09:21.143 --> 00:09:24.580
Count the small values to check it.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:26.757
Next: median-of-three

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.331
a deterministic quicksort that

00:09:29.414 --> 00:09:31.490
is very fast in practice.

